<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">18 November in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 18 November, from 326 to 1918.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">326 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,700 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Old St Peter's Basilica is dedicated, by tradition</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">According to church tradition, Pope Sylvester I dedicated Constantine's basilica over the site of St Peter's tomb on the Vatican Hill. It stood for some 1,200 years before being replaced by the present church.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1210">1210 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">816 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Innocent III excommunicates Emperor Otto IV</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> excommunicated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> Otto IV, who had demanded that he annul the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concordat-of-worms">Concordat of Worms</a>. In 1211 princes of the empire, with the pope's consent, elected the Staufen king Frederick as King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1302">1302 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">724 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Boniface VIII issues the bull Unam sanctam</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> issued the bull Unam sanctam, declaring the pope supreme head of the Church and submission to him necessary for salvation. It was the definitive statement of the late medieval theory of the pope's temporal and spiritual supremacy.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1307">1307 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">719 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">William Tell shoots the apple, according to legend</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Swiss tradition dates to this day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a>'s shot at an apple on his son's head in Altdorf, ordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> bailiff Gessler. Historians regard the tale as legend, but it became a symbol of Swiss liberty.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1477">1477 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">549 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Caxton completes the first dated book printed in England</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At Westminster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-caxton">William Caxton</a> finished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> The Dictes or Sayengis of the Philosophres, the first book printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to carry a date. Printing in English helped standardise the language.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1601">1601 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">425 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Tiryaki Hasan Pasha routs the Habsburg besiegers of Nagykanizsa</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Ottoman commander Tiryaki Hasan Pasha led a surprise charge that routed the army of Archduke Ferdinand, the future Emperor Ferdinand II, besieging the fortress of Nagykanizsa in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The Austrian army was driven back and lost 47 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannons</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand II</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1626">1626 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">400 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Urban VIII consecrates the new St Peter's Basilica</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Exactly 1,300 years after the traditional dedication of the first basilica, Urban VIII consecrated the new St Peter's. Its design involved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Bramante</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> and Maderno over 120 years.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1730 CE <span class="tih-ago">296 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The future Frederick the Great is pardoned by his father</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> was granted a royal pardon by his father and released from confinement, although he remained stripped of his military rank. He was kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a> to study statecraft and administration.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1918">1918 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">108 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Latvia proclaims its independence</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The People's Council in Riga declared an independent Latvian state. After a war of independence it remained free until Soviet occupation in 1940, regaining independence in 1991.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 18 November <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/lamoral-count-of-egmont.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</span><span class="tp">1522–1568</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-louis-ii-count-of-hanau-munzenberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/philip-louis-ii-count-of-hanau-munzenberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Philip Louis II, Count of Hanau-Münzenberg</span><span class="tp">1576–1612</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/luis-somoza-debayle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/luis-somoza-debayle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Luis Somoza Debayle</span><span class="tp">1922–1967</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/margaret-atwood"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/margaret-atwood.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Margaret Atwood</span><span class="tp">b. 1939</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 18 November <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/albert-the-bear.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Albert the Bear</span><span class="tp">c. 1100–1170</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/bessarion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bessarion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Bessarion</span><span class="tp">1403–1472</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/agustin-gamarra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Agustín Gamarra</span><span class="tp">1785–1841</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/niels-bohr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/niels-bohr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Niels Bohr</span><span class="tp">1885–1962</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Kurt Schuschnigg</span><span class="tp">1897–1977</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gustav-husak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Gustáv Husák</span><span class="tp">1913–1991</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
